---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import path from "node:path";
import {
  DEFAULT_WINDOW_DAYS,
  MIN_STRATUM_N,
  DRAWS,
  SIGMA_INFLATION,
  TOO_CLOSE_TO_CALL,
} from "../../../../scripts/lib/local-byelection-model.mjs";

const ROOT = process.cwd();
const meta = JSON.parse(
  readFileSync(path.join(ROOT, "data/contests/local-byelections/_meta.json"), "utf8")
);
const bt = meta.backtest;
const rec = bt.recent;
const cal = meta.calibration_table || [];
const cmp = meta.method_comparison ?? null;
const pct = (x: number | null | undefined, dp = 0) =>
  typeof x === "number" ? (x * 100).toFixed(dp) : "n/a";
const pp = (x: number | null | undefined) => (typeof x === "number" ? x.toFixed(1) : "n/a");

const description = `How the council by-election projections are built: the ward's own last result, moved by swing measured only from comparable by-elections (same era, same Reform-entry condition), with the full ${rec.n}-contest record and the pooled comparison published.`;
---

<BaseLayout title="Methodology, council by-elections" description={description}>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
    <h1>The council by-election model</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      One method, never blended: the ward's own last ordinary result, moved by the median swing
      measured across recent council by-elections we hold a like-for-like baseline for. This page
      is the full design, including the parts each contest page only summarises, and the record
      that keeps it honest.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section prose-block">
    <h2>The baseline</h2>
    <p>
      Every projection starts from the ward's own last ordinary result, paired at ward level so the
      starting shares are the shares this exact electorate produced. A contest without a
      like-for-like baseline gets no number: the page still exists, shows the candidates and the
      vacancy, and says why we will not project it. As of the last rebuild the paired corpus holds
      {meta.corpus_size} by-elections, from {meta.corpus_oldest} to {meta.corpus_newest}.
    </p>

    <h2>The swing, and why it is stratified</h2>
    <p>
      The move applied to the baseline is the median per-party shift measured across recent council
      by-elections, in log-odds space, over a {DEFAULT_WINDOW_DAYS}-day window. But not across all
      of them at once. Two things make raw pooling wrong:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Era.</strong> A ward last fought before the Reform realignment has already absorbed
        a huge structural swing that a ward last fought in May 2025 has not. The corpus is split by
        when the baseline was fought: before the realignment, around the 2024 general election, and
        since May 2025.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Whether Reform UK is entering or defending.</strong> A party standing in a ward for
        the first time mechanically depresses every other party's share, so the shift measured for
        Labour where Reform newly entered is not comparable to one where Reform already stood. Each
        era is therefore split again: Reform entering against Reform already standing.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each contest is projected from the stratum it belongs to, and every contest page names its
      comparison group and the number of by-elections behind it. A stratum thinner than
      {MIN_STRATUM_N} contests falls back to the pooled estimate rather than pretending a handful
      of results is a measurement. A party standing in a ward for the first time starts from the
      share first-time entrants actually achieve in the corpus, never from national polling.
    </p>

    {cmp && (
      <>
        <h2>Does the stratification earn its place?</h2>
        <p>{cmp.note}</p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Method</th><th>Contests</th><th>Winner called</th><th>Mean absolute error</th><th>Excluding too close</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Stratified (published)</strong></td>
                <td>{cmp.stratified.n}</td>
                <td>{cmp.stratified.winner_called} ({pct(cmp.stratified.winner_called_pct)}%)</td>
                <td>{pp(cmp.stratified.mae_pp)}pp</td>
                <td>{cmp.stratified.confident_called} of {cmp.stratified.confident_n}</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Pooled</td>
                <td>{cmp.pooled.n}</td>
                <td>{cmp.pooled.winner_called} ({pct(cmp.pooled.winner_called_pct)}%)</td>
                <td>{pp(cmp.pooled.mae_pp)}pp</td>
                <td>{cmp.pooled.confident_called} of {cmp.pooled.confident_n}</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Same corpus, same window, recomputed on every rebuild. If the pooled row ever beats the
          stratified row, this page will say so, and the method should change.
        </p>
      </>
    )}

    <h2>Uncertainty, and what the probability means</h2>
    <p>
      The projection is run through {DRAWS.toLocaleString("en-GB")} simulations with per-party
      noise scaled from the corpus and inflated by a factor of {SIGMA_INFLATION}. That factor is
      not a guess: the raw simulation was overconfident against the record (contests it put above
      90% were won about 73% of the time), so the spread was widened on the full backtest until
      stated probability matched observed frequency, holding the Brier score at its minimum. The
      win probability on each page is simply the share of simulations a party wins.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The <strong>strongest lane is the party that wins the most simulations</strong>, not the one
        with the highest central share; in tight races a fatter-variance lane can win more draws on
        a lower central projection, and the probability is the honest ranking of a simulation.
      </li>
      <li>
        Below {pct(TOO_CLOSE_TO_CALL)}% the page says <strong>too close to call</strong> and names
        no favourite.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What the model cannot see</h2>
    <p>
      Candidate quality and local name recognition, which decide more council by-elections than
      national swing does. Ward-level campaigns, and which parties actually knocked on doors.
      Turnout, which routinely falls below a quarter of the electorate. This is why the record
      below matters more than any single number.
    </p>

    <h2>Two deliberate refusals</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The previous holder is never derived.</strong> Defections, suspensions and
        independents make "who held the seat" unsafe to infer from results data, so gain and hold
        language appears only where a holder has been verified by hand against primary sources.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The census profile is not an input.</strong> Each page shows the ward against the
        median ward because it helps a reader; the projection works from votes.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The record</h2>
    <p>
      Tested by projecting every council by-election since {bt.recent_since} from only what was
      known beforehand: across {rec.n} contests it called the winner {rec.winner_called} times
      ({pct(rec.winner_called_pct)}%), with a mean absolute error of {pp(rec.mae_pp)}pp per party.
      Restricted to contests it did not call too close, it was right {rec.confident_called} of
      {rec.confident_n} times. Stated probability against what happened:
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>When we said</th><th>Contests</th><th>Average claim</th><th>We were right</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {cal.map((b: any) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{pct(b.from)} to {pct(Math.min(b.to, 1))}%</td>
              <td>{b.n}</td>
              <td>{pct(b.mean_stated)}%</td>
              <td>{pct(b.observed)}%</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The whole pipeline reruns nightly, and this page's figures are read from the same file the
      projections are written to, so they cannot drift apart. Every contest, one page each:
      <a href="/by-elections/local/">the live list</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .table-scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 0.75rem 0 1rem; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: 0.45rem 0.9rem 0.45rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); font-variant-numeric: lining-nums tabular-nums; }
</style>
